---
title: Slamhaus
description: Build static sites and interactive browser applications with Markdown and C++.
---
# Slamhaus

## Markdown in. C++ out. Ship something weird.

Slamhaus is a command-line static-site compiler for game developers, systems
people, and anyone who would rather build a tiny tool than wrestle a frontend
stack. Write ordinary Markdown, add typed UI when you need interaction, and
embed C++ that compiles to WebAssembly.

[Getting started](getting-started.md) · [View examples](examples.md) · [Open the live showcase](showcase.slam) · [API cheatsheet](syntax.md)

No package maze. No hydration tax. No handwritten JavaScript for supported
interaction. Just a compiler, a project folder, and a `dist/` directory you can
put anywhere.

## Why Slamhaus?

### Built like a game-engine subsystem

The compiler is modular C++17: parsing, validation, routing, rendering, the
language server, and the WebAssembly bridge are explicit systems. It is fast to
run, straightforward to inspect, and comfortable to extend.

### Markdown stays the source of truth

Portable `.md` remains portable GFM. `.slam` adds only the things a site needs:
metadata, typed UI, and optional C++ behavior. Your content is still readable
in a text editor, a diff, or a terminal.

### Interactive without the frontend ceremony

Name a component, bind an event, write a typed function, and let Slamhaus
generate the DOM IDs, module manifest, argument conversion, and accessible
output. The browser gets the runtime; you get to think in C++.

### Static by default, capable when needed

Landing pages, docs, portfolios, release notes, and blogs emit plain HTML and
CSS. Add Emscripten only to the pages that actually need it: calculators,
tools, toys, canvas experiments, and game-like interfaces.

### Deterministic and deployable

Routes, links, partials, assets, metadata, and accessibility checks happen at
build time. The result is a complete site in `dist/`, ready for GitHub Pages,
an object bucket, a CDN, or a USB stick.

## A small language

Pages use `.md` when they should remain portable and `.slam` when they need
Slam UI or executable code. Site configuration lives in `slamhaus.yaml`.

```slam-ui
Number Left("Left") { value = 2; }
Number Right("Right") { value = 3; }
Button Calculate("Add") { on Click => Add(Left, Right) -> Result; }
Output Result("Ready");
Element Signal() {
  tag = "div";
  class = "ascii-stage home-signal";
  Text Frame("S L A M // *");
};
```

The complete working version is on the [live showcase](showcase.slam).

```cpp wasm
auto Add(int32 A, int32 B) -> int32 {
  return A + B;
}
```

## Start a project

```sh
slamhaus init my-site
cd my-site
slamhaus check --strict
slamhaus build
slamhaus serve
```

## The short version

```cpp
content       -> Markdown and Slam UI you can read
compiler      -> routes, HTML, CSS, diagnostics, and modules
typed C++     -> optional WebAssembly behavior
dist/         -> a boring, portable website you can ship
```

Slamhaus is a good fit when you want:

- a personal site or studio landing page without a JavaScript framework;
- documentation that stays close to source control and the command line;
- game-dev-flavoured UI and browser toys written in familiar C++;
- a small, inspectable compiler instead of a cloud build service;
- accessible semantic HTML with responsive controls from the start;
- a static deployment story that remains cheap, cacheable, and portable.

It is deliberately not a full game engine, CMS, SPA framework, or proprietary
renderer. Bring your own rendering technology when you need one; Slamhaus
handles the site, the document, and the bridge to the browser.
